.TH ENCLO 1 "2026-06-07" "enclo" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
enclo \- modular rootless container launcher
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B enclo
[\fITARGET\fR]
.I COMMAND
[\fIARGS\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B enclo
manages Podman containers using a declarative set of pluggable security modules.
Each module contributes environment variables, volume mounts, and podman arguments
before the container starts. Modules that require sidecar processes (secrets
service, D-Bus proxy) are started alongside the container and torn down when it
exits.
.PP
Container configs live at
.IR ~/.config/enclo/containers/<NAME>.toml .
Pass
.B \-\-config
to override the name-based lookup.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Target selectors are mutually exclusive and can appear before or after commands.
Positional target names are not accepted.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-directory " " \fIPATH\fR
Search this directory and its parents for .enclo.toml. Error if none is found;
never fall back to a standalone central profile for an explicit directory.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-container " " \fINAME\fR
Select the standalone central profile ~/.config/enclo/containers/<NAME>.toml.
NAME is the profile filename stem, not the Podman container.name setting.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Select an explicit configuration file, bypassing workspace discovery.
.PP
Without a selector, lifecycle commands discover the nearest workspace configuration,
otherwise using the central default profile. The implicit choice is printed to
stderr. A discovered invalid, unreadable or untrusted file fails without fallback.
Status without a selector lists all instances. Serve accepts no target selectors;
untrust accepts only --directory, defaulting to the current directory.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS serve
Start the enclo daemon.
.PP
.B enclo serve
.PP
On Linux, registers a D-Bus session service at
.BR org.enclo.Manager .
On macOS, listens on a Unix socket at
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/enclo/daemon.sock .
A running daemon is replaced. Usually managed by the
.BR enclo.service (5)
systemd user unit.
.SS run
Start a container directly, in the foreground, without a daemon.
.PP
.B enclo run
[\fITARGET\fR]
.PP
Orchestrates all enabled modules, starts the container, and blocks until the
container exits.
.SS start
Ask the daemon to start a container.
.PP
.B enclo start
[\fITARGET\fR]
.PP
.SS stop
Ask the daemon to stop a container.
.PP
.B enclo stop
[\fITARGET\fR]
[\fB\-\-force\fR]
.TP
.B \-\-force
Stop even if there are active
.BR podman-exec (1)
sessions attached to the container.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-force ,
the command fails if any exec sessions are open.
.SS restart
Ask the daemon to stop then start a container.
.PP
.B enclo restart
[\fITARGET\fR]
[\fB\-\-force\fR]
.TP
.B \-\-force
Restart even if there are active exec sessions.
.PP
.SS enter
Enter a running container, starting it via the daemon first if necessary.
.PP
.B enclo enter
[\fITARGET\fR]
[\fB\-\-\fR \fICMD\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]]
.PP
.I CMD
defaults to the
.B exec_shell
from the container config (default:
.BR bash ).
.PP
If the container is not running, the daemon is asked to start it before execing in.
The working directory inside the container defaults to the current directory on the
host, or
.B exec_workdir
from the config if set.
.SS exec
Exec into an already-running container without contacting the daemon.
.PP
.B enclo exec
[\fITARGET\fR]
[\fB\-\-\fR \fICMD\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]]
.PP
Errors if the container is not running. Does not attempt to start it.
.I CMD
defaults to
.B exec_shell
from config.
.SS reload
Send SIGHUP to the modules of a running container that support hot-reload.
.PP
.B enclo reload
[\fITARGET\fR]
.PP
Currently reloads the secrets service (re-reads store config and proxy rules).
The container is not restarted.
.SS status
Show container status.
.PP
.B enclo status
[\fITARGET\fR]
.PP
With no selector, lists all known containers and their states.
With a selector, shows detailed
status including module health.
.SS setup-volume
Create missing directories inside the home named volume with correct ownership.
.PP
.B enclo setup-volume
[\fITARGET\fR]
[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be created without making any changes.
.PP
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/enclo/containers/<NAME>.toml
Container config file (Linux).
.TP
.I ~/.config/enclo/containers/<NAME>.toml
Container config file (macOS).
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/enclo/<name>/
Per-container runtime state: module sockets, exec-env file.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/enclo/daemon.sock
Daemon socket (macOS).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# Start the daemon (usually done by systemd)
enclo serve

# Start a container via the daemon
enclo start -C dev

# Open a shell in the container
enclo enter -C dev

# Run a one-off command without starting via daemon
enclo exec -C dev -- git status

# Reload secrets config without restarting
enclo reload -C dev

# Check container status
enclo status
enclo status -C dev

# Stop the container
enclo stop -C dev

# Force-stop (ignores open exec sessions)
enclo stop -C dev --force

# Run a container directly (no daemon, blocks)
enclo run -C dev

# Use an explicit config file
enclo --config ~/my-container.toml run
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR enclo-container (1),
.BR podman (1),
.BR podman-run (1),
.BR xdg-dbus-proxy (1),
.BR gpg-agent (1)
.PP
Full documentation:
.I docs/configuration.md
and
.I docs/commands.md
in the enclo source tree.

.SH WORKSPACE CONFIGURATION
Without an explicit target selector, lifecycle commands search the current
and parent directories for the nearest .enclo.toml. The file permits only
container.from (the central profile name), container.directory (the workspace
mount destination, default /workspace or the central profile's directory), and
container.home.init.command_after when managed homes are enabled centrally.
The containing directory is mounted read/write. Each workspace has a separate
container identity and runtime services; central persistent volumes remain shared.
.PP
Workspace configurations require interactive approval, stored against their
canonical directory and content hash under $XDG_STATE_HOME/enclo/trust
(default ~/.local/state/enclo/trust). Changed files require renewed approval.
Run enclo untrust in a workspace to revoke approval. Changed container configuration
requires enclo restart. Status without a selector lists all instances.

.SH MANAGED HOMES
Set container.home.enabled=true in the central profile to create an empty,
persistent named home volume per container instance. Remove existing home
mounts before enabling it. container.home.init.command accepts a multiline
shell script; a local config may append container.home.init.command_after.
Local approval displays and authorizes that script.
.PP
Scripts run with sh -eu as the normal user after services start. Each successful
stage is recorded separately in the home volume. Failed stages retry on the next
launch; completed scripts do not rerun automatically when edited. The per-script
container.home.init.timeout_secs defaults to 600.
.PP
Use enclo home init [TARGET] --rerun on a running instance to explicitly rerun both
scripts. Without --rerun only pending scripts execute. Script failure stops the
container. Home contents and completion records survive container recreation.

.SH WORKSPACE NETWORKS
Set container.workspace_network = true in a central profile or local .enclo.toml
to create an identity-scoped bridge network before secrets setup. This overrides
container.network. Networks persist across stops and are reused on restart.
Workspace identities use enclo-workspace-<hash>; old enclo-project identities and
home volumes are not automatically migrated.
